            <ArticleTitle>Cancer pain education for physicians in practice: establishing a new paradigm.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>364-71</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Traditional education methods for physicians have failed to impact positively upon cancer pain clinical practice. This article presents a new paradigm for physician cancer pain education based on principles of adult learning and physician behavior theory. Three key issues that weave together education and institutionalization issues are reviewed: (a) how to make physicians aware that their current practice behavior is less than optimal; (b) how to match education objectives with appropriate education formats; and (c) how to change pain management practices without imposing new burdens on physician practice.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
